---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="Error handling">
  <p>
    The API uses standard HTTP status codes. Most error responses are JSON, and a few are plain text.
    This page covers what each one means and how to respond.
  </p>

  <h2>Status codes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Code</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
        <th>Action</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>200</code></td>
        <td>Success</td>
        <td>Parse the response body</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>401</code></td>
        <td>Unauthorized</td>
        <td>Check your API key, or the request was a cache miss</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>404</code></td>
        <td>Not found</td>
        <td>Lyrics not available for this track</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>422</code></td>
        <td>Unprocessable</td>
        <td>Neither song nor artist was sent</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>429</code></td>
        <td>Too many requests</td>
        <td>Rate limited. Check the Retry-After header</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>500</code></td>
        <td>Server error</td>
        <td>Upstream failure. Retry with backoff</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>503</code></td>
        <td>Service unavailable</td>
        <td>The server is serving only cached lyrics and the query is not cached. Retry later</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Error response format</h2>
  <p>JSON error responses always have <code>error</code>. Some also have <code>message</code>:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "error": "Short error description",
  "message": "Longer explanation with suggested action"
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Two errors are plain text (<code>Content-Type: text/plain</code>) instead: <code>422</code>, and the
    <code>429</code> sent when both rate limit tiers are exhausted. Check the <code>Content-Type</code>
    header before you parse the body as JSON.
  </p>

  <h2>Common errors</h2>

  <h3>Missing parameters (422)</h3>
  <pre><code>Song name or artist name not provided</code></pre>
  <p>
    Plain text. Sent only when both <code>s</code> (song) and <code>a</code> (artist) are missing. Always send
    all four parameters anyway: the cache key includes album and duration, so a request without them often
    misses the cached entry and, without an API key, returns <code>401</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Lyrics not found (404)</h3>
  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "error": "Lyrics not available for this track"
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    No lyrics exist for this song. The result is cached for 7 days (negative cache).
    Check <code>X-Cache-Status: NEGATIVE_HIT</code> to know if this is a cached result.
  </p>

  <h3>API key required (401)</h3>
  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "error": "API key required",
  "message": "Uncached queries require a valid API key via X-API-Key header"
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The requested lyrics are not cached and the server requires an API key for fresh fetches.
    First check that the request sends album and duration: the cache key includes both, so a request
    without them often misses an entry that is cached.
    Keys aren't being issued right now (see <a href="/docs/authentication">Authentication</a>).
    If you have a key, pass it via <code>X-API-Key</code>. Otherwise, play the song once through the
    <a href="https://better-lyrics.boidu.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Better Lyrics</a> extension
    to prime the cache, then retry.
  </p>

  <h3>Rate limited (429)</h3>
  <p>When both rate limit tiers are exhausted, the body is plain text and <code>Retry-After</code> is <code>1</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>Too Many Requests</code></pre>
  <p>When only the cached tier is left and the query is not cached, the body is JSON and <code>Retry-After</code> is <code>60</code>:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "error": "Rate limit exceeded. This request requires cached data, but no cache is available for this query.",
  "message": "Please try again later or reduce your request rate."
}`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Check the <code>Retry-After</code> header and <code>X-RateLimit-Type</code> to see which case you hit.
    See <a href="/docs/rate-limiting">Rate limiting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Circuit breaker</h2>
  <p>
    The API puts a circuit breaker in front of the upstream provider.
    When the upstream fails repeatedly, the breaker "opens" so the failures
    do not cascade.
  </p>

  <h3>Circuit breaker states</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>State</th>
        <th>Behavior</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>CLOSED</code></td>
        <td>Normal operation. Requests go through</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>OPEN</code></td>
        <td>Tripped. Requests fail fast, and stale cache is served if available</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>HALF-OPEN</code></td>
        <td>Testing recovery. A limited number of requests go through</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>While the breaker is open</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Cached responses are still served (both positive and stale cache)</li>
    <li>Fresh fetch requests fail fast instead of timing out</li>
    <li>Every so often the breaker lets a test request through to check whether upstream has recovered</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Retry strategies</h2>

  <h3>For 429 (rate limited)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Read the <code>Retry-After</code> header</li>
    <li>Wait the specified number of seconds</li>
    <li>Retry the request</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>For 500 (server error)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Use exponential backoff</li>
    <li>Start with a 1 second delay</li>
    <li>Double the delay on each retry (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s...)</li>
    <li>Cap at a maximum delay (e.g., 60 seconds)</li>
    <li>Give up after a maximum number of retries (e.g., 5)</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>For 404 (not found)</h3>
  <p>
    Do not retry immediately. The negative result is cached for 7 days.
    If you believe lyrics should exist, use the <code>/revalidate</code> endpoint
    with an API key to force a fresh lookup.
  </p>

  <h2>Example error handling (JavaScript)</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-javascript">{`async function fetchLyrics(song, artist, album, duration) {
  const url = \`https://api.betterlyrics.org/getLyrics?s=\${encodeURIComponent(song)}&a=\${encodeURIComponent(artist)}&al=\${encodeURIComponent(album)}&d=\${duration}\`;

  const response = await fetch(url);

  if (response.ok) {
    return await response.json();
  }

  const isJson = response.headers.get('Content-Type')?.includes('application/json');
  const error = isJson ? await response.json() : { error: await response.text() };

  switch (response.status) {
    case 404:
      // Lyrics not available - cache this locally
      return { notFound: true, cached: response.headers.get('X-Cache-Status') === 'NEGATIVE_HIT' };

    case 429:
      // Rate limited - get retry time
      const retryAfter = parseInt(response.headers.get('Retry-After') || '60');
      throw new Error(\`Rate limited. Retry after \${retryAfter} seconds.\`);

    case 401:
      // Auth required for cache miss
      throw new Error('API key required for uncached queries');

    default:
      throw new Error(error.message || error.error || 'Unknown error');
  }
}`}</code></pre>
</DocsLayout>
